# RULES — Repository Law & Core Principles
File: openOODA/RULES.oot

These governance rules do not change. They apply across all repository directories.

## 1. Basic Rules

The 23 laws below do not change. They apply across all repository directories.

### 1.1 First Principles & Specification Derivation (Pillar 1)
  Identify the root cause of a defect. Implement structural repairs rather than temporary workarounds.
  Derive algorithms, data models, and grammar directly from hardware specifications.
  Base implementations on IEEE 754 floats, NIST Known Answer Tests, and IETF RFCs.
  Do not copy unverified code or wrap incomplete stubs.
  Treat all unproven claims as defective.
  Anti-Shortcut & Real Pipeline Rule:
    Compiler and runtime code must execute genuine AST node traversal and typed intermediate representations.
    Prohibit substring matching, regex heuristics, and canned stub defaults in lowering pipelines.
    Connecting interfaces without wiring underlying execution paths is a governance violation.

### 1.2 Boyd's Energy-Maneuverability Engine & Subtractive Design (Pillar 2)
  Col. John Boyd's Energy-Maneuverability formula governs performance:
    E-M = ((T - D) / W) * V
  Limitation of Instruction-Level Optimization: Kinematic instruction tuning cannot detect whole-system state drift.
  Informational OODA Transition: Elevate the loop from CPU cycles to AST observation and SMT contract orientation.
  The Subtractive Optimization Law:
    Improve software systems by systematic subtraction.
    Remove garbage collection pauses, ambient authority, null pointers, and dynamic dispatch.
    Maximize Thrust (T): Maximize instruction throughput, SIMD vectorization, and direct ELF64/WASM emission.
    Eliminate Drag (D): Use linear arenas with O(1) bulk resets to prevent heap allocation overhead.
    Eliminate ARC retain and release overhead.
    Use static SMT proofs to elide dynamic contract evaluations.
    Minimize Weight (W): Minimize binary size, stack frame depth, and external dependencies.
    Accelerate Velocity (V): Maximize rebuild speed, incremental AST typechecking rate, and agent loop turnover.

### 1.3 Action on Every Turn
  Execute productive work on every agent turn.
  If you cannot complete a task, document the specific technical reason.
  A TODO item without a corresponding backlog entry is not a valid plan.
  Finish the current milestone goals before starting unplanned secondary tasks.

### 1.4 Honesty & Complete Delivery
  If a task remains incomplete, document the unfinished components explicitly.
  Do not leave trailing TODO markers, incomplete stubs, or uncommitted test gates.
  Verify every component end-to-end and certify with a double-run test before marking complete.
  Adversarial Falsification Before Victory:
    Do not declare a feature or bug fix complete without writing and executing a negative-trust test.
    The test must actively probe and break boundary conditions (e.g. oversized dimensions, invalid types, or non-target sources).

### 1.5 Security, Boundary Physics & Fail-Closed Behavior
  All operations must fail closed: zero panics, zero unhandled exceptions, and explicit Result returns.
  Zero Ambient Authority: Code possesses no implicit access to filesystem, network, or OS processes.
  Follow capability grant matrices in openOODA/rfcs/0001-capability-security.oot. Grant only the exact capability token required.
  Boundary Physics Limits:
    - Rice's Theorem (SMT Decidability): Restrict formal contract verification to decidable logics (QF_LIA, QF_BV). Fail closed on timeout.
    - CAP Theorem (Monotonic Leases): Prioritize consistency and safety over availability during network partitions.
    - Landauer's Principle (Minimal Entropy): Reclaim memory through linear arena bulk resets and zero-leak ARC.
  Do not place secret keys or passwords in source code or log files.

### 1.6 Multi-Agent Coordination & Swarm Safety (Pillar 6)
  Each code change must decrease line count or complexity while maintaining all test verifications.
  Autonomous swarms operate on stationary workspace topology (Delta_path = 0).
  Agent self-replication and identity mutation are strictly blocked fail-closed.
  Hierarchical Loop Stratification:
    Stabilize lower-level loops as deterministic libraries:
    - Gen 1 (Silicon Hardware): Bare-metal page tables, SIMD registers, and interrupt controllers.
    - Gen 2 (Compiler & IR): AST Merkle digests, SSA mem2reg, and direct machine code emission.
    - Gen 3 (Capability Runtime): Linear arena allocators, bulk resets, deterministic ARC, and Landlock.
    - Gen 4 (Language & Types): Refinement types, typestates, SECRET taint tracking, and SMT contracts.
    - Gen 5 (Agent Teams) & Gen 6 (Autonomous Synthesis): High-level task planning and code synthesis.
  Coordinate across multi-agent teams through continuous git and disk awareness.
  Re-read files before editing and prevent file clobbering.
  Make atomic commits with clear handoff reports.

### 1.7 Verification Proofs
  When you build a feature, prove that the implementation works correctly.
  Prove that the implementation terminates safely when an error occurs.
  A test that always passes does not provide proof.
  If a task fails mid-execution, restore the repository to a clean state.
  Follow the adversarial verification protocols in WORKFLOW.oot.
  Verified on-disk features are recorded by the per-repo shipped proof scripts. The
  governance repo has no shipped ledger; each tactical/operational repo records its own.

### 1.8 Code Quality & Line Limits (Pillar 2)
  Every file must contain a maximum of 256 lines. Split large files along domain seams.
  Free all allocated memory. Use linear arenas with bulk resets for temporary allocations.
  Delete debug files, obsolete code, and temporary artifacts when work is complete.
  Zero intermediate artifacts (*.tmp.c, *.protos.c, *.tu.c, *.bin, *.txt) permitted in openOODA/std/.
  Ensure that implementation behavior matches specification documentation.

### 1.9 Incremental Modifications
  Make small, focused modifications. Small changes are easier to test and verify.

### 1.10 Negative Trust & Adversarial Validation (Pillar 3)
  Assume that unverified stubs, docstrings, and tests contain defects until proven.
  Apply active falsification: Test code with corrupted tokens, invalid inputs, and boundary values.
  Deploy the 8-Dimensional Red Team roles defined in `practices/8d-red-team.oot`:
    - Red 1 (Heuristic Hunter / e1): Lowering AST integrity and keyword decoy immunity.
    - Red 2 (Boundary Falsifier / e2): Dynamic parameter bounds and line limit verification.
    - Red 3 (Data-Path Tracer / e3): Genuine execution wiring and fallback rejection.
    - Red 4 (Hermetic Jailor / e4): Capability isolation and Landlock sandboxing.
    - Red 5 (Temporal Saboteur / e5): Concurrency races, stream synchronization, and replay drift.
    - Red 6 (Drag Assassin / e6): Algorithmic complexity, arena leaks, and GC pause elimination.
    - Red 7 (Semantic Subverter / e7): AST mutant kill rate (>= 95%) and non-vacuous contracts.
    - Red 8 (Byzantine Infiltrator / e8): Constant-time cryptography (|t_Welch| < 4.5) and RASP rollback.
  Follow the 8-Gate Blocking Veto Protocol in `practices/8d-red-team.oot` for candidate code.
  Follow the Double-Run Law: Execute `./bin/ooda run <test.oo>` twice in fresh processes.
  Do not trust plain board text; execute verification scripts directly.

### 1.11 ASD-STE100 & Academy 4-Element Headers (Pillar 4)
  All agent text and documentation must follow ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English.
  Every product `.oo` file begins with a mandatory 4-element Academy header:
    `// # <Title>`, `// Logline:`, `// Setup:`, `// Beats:`.
  Every `.oot` process board begins with `# <Title>`, `File:` (2-line clean header; Floor/Thrust/Patch SSoT in ooda.pkg).
  Write short sentences. Put exactly one thought in each sentence.
  Use approved words from the openOODA dictionary. Give each word exactly one meaning.
  Use the active voice. Do not use slang, jokes, metaphors, or marketing terms.
  State technical facts and mathematical invariants directly and quantitatively.
  Explain each technical term the first time you use it.
  State four items in each task summary:
    1. What changed.
    2. Why the change is necessary.
    3. What work remains incomplete.
    4. What step to execute next.
  Consult FORMAT.oot for document layout rules.

### 1.12 Algorithmic Selection
  If two viable implementations exist, evaluate both solutions with benchmarks.
  Select the faster and simpler implementation. Delete the unused implementation.

### 1.13 Formal RFC Template Compliance (Pillar 7)
  All RFC proposals in `openOODA/rfcs/` must adhere to `0000-template.oot`.
  Required sections: RFC ID, Title, Author, Status, Summary, Motivation, Design, Security, and Testing.
  Implemented RFCs must contain concrete adversarial test suites.
  When writing networking code, adhere strictly to published IETF RFC specifications.
  Record the relevant RFC number in the source file header.

### 1.14 Pure .oo/.oot File Laws, Two-Binary & Clean Workspaces (Pillars 1, 8, 9, 10)
  All source code, tools, proofs, fixers, and test harnesses must use `.oo` files.
  All process boards, research papers, RFCs, and specifications must use `.oot` files.
  Strictly Prohibited:
    - Never create `.py` files. Python is unauthorized across all directories.
    - Never create `.sh` files, except the two authorized host scripts.
    - Never create `.js`, `.ts`, `.rb`, or `.pl` files outside editor extensions.
    - Never create `.md` files, except the top-level `README.md` of the openOODA governance repo.
  Two-Binary Architecture (Pillar 8): The compiler (`oodac`) is decoupled from the workflow driver (`ooda`).
  Gen 1 C/H Substrate Isolation (Pillar 9): All low-level C shims (`chs_rt_*.c`, `chs_rt.h`) strictly confined to the ooda/ monorepo runtime. Zero C header leaks into openOODA/std/.
  Clean Workspaces (Pillar 10): Zero unmanaged/unheaded `.oo` files in roots; zero struct trailing commas.
  Polyrepo directory naming responsibilities (each repo owns its own subtree):
    `ooda/oodac/*.oo`    — Compiler implementation modules (ooda/ monorepo).
    `ooda/runtime/*`     — Gen 1 C shims and Landlock sandbox (ooda/ monorepo).
    `ooda/cli/*.oo`      — Product CLI commands and entrypoint (cli/ repo).
    `openOODA/std/*.oo`  — Standard library modules, 8 functional domains (std/ repo).
    `cli/`, `lsp/`, `mcp/`, `opm/` — Operational drivers and protocol servers (own repos).
  Every `.oo` file must contain a 4-element Academy header and contain <= 256 lines.
  Two host scripts are permitted: `bootstrap/oodac_pure_build` and `install.sh`.

### 1.15 Automated Enforcement
  Each tactical/operational repo owns its own governance linters and tests. The
  governance repo enforces only its own subtree: 256-line cap, file-type bans, and
  the 8-D Red Team pre-commit gates described in `practices/8d-red-team.oot`.
  Any commit that introduces unauthorized file types or oversized files fails closed.

### 1.17 Power Law 80/20 Leverage
  Concentrate architecture efforts on the 20% high-leverage primitives:
    - SSA mem2reg variable promotion.
    - Flat closures with explicit capture environments.
    - Direct machine code emission for ELF64 and WebAssembly.
    - Linear arena memory allocators with bulk resets.

### 1.18 Capability Architecture & Zero Ambient Authority (Pillar 5)
  Maintain foundational architectural security advantages:
    - Compile-time SMT check elision to remove redundant runtime checks.
    - Zero ambient authority capability model with 14 explicit unforgeable tokens.
    - Self-hosted compiler bootstrapping directly emitting native binaries.

### 1.19 .oot File Naming Conventions
  Two naming rules determine document provenance by letter case:
  1. ALLCAPS.oot — Owner-Authored Canonical Files:
     - Apply ALLCAPS naming to the 13 canonical process boards in `openOODA/`.
     - Apply ALLCAPS naming to backlog specification chapters in `openOODA/backlog/`.
     - Apply ALLCAPS naming to files that a human engineer writes and maintains.
  2. lowercase.oot — Agent-Generated Files:
     - Apply lowercase naming to all files in `audit/` and `.agents/` directories.
     - Apply lowercase naming to RFC specifications in `rfcs/` (e.g. `0001-name.oot`).
     - Apply lowercase naming to session rollups, test reports, and audit logs.

### 1.20 Three Fundamental Operational Laws
  All compiler passes, agent workflows, and runtime subsystems must obey three laws:
  1. Law of Substratum Integrity: Fix low-level memory and concurrency drag before building high-level orchestration.
  2. Law of Hermetic Encapsulation: Treat each subsystem as an encapsulated module with explicit capability tokens.
  3. Law of Bounded Promotion: Require formal SMT proofs or invariant checks before promoting dynamic code.

### 1.21 Generational Semantic Versioning & 80/20 Floor Rule
  All release artifacts and manifests obey Generational Semantic Versioning:
  - Version Syntax: `v<Floor>.<Thrust>.<Patch>` (Verified State: `v2.11.0` from `ooda.pkg`).
  - Floor (F): Increments on breaking changes to memory invariants or capability ABI.
  - Thrust (T): Increments on performance improvements and non-breaking feature additions.
  - Patch (P): Increments on bug repairs and security remediation.
  - Power Law 80/20 Floor Rule: Increment Floor only when the 20% core substrate changes.

### 1.22 Standard Library Reuse Directive
  Do not create new files in `openOODA/std/` unless the user explicitly requests standard library expansion.
  Inspect and reuse existing modules in `openOODA/std/` (8 functional domains, 173 ANCHOR.oo) before implementing new utility code.


### 1.23 Three-Layer Architecture (Strategic, Operational, Tactical) — Polyrepo
openOODA operates as a 3-Tier Multi-Repository Architecture across 9 repos:
- **Strategic Governance** (`openOODA/openOODA/`): this repo. Process boards, RFCs, the 23 laws. No compiler code.
- **Operational Logistics** (`openOODA/cli/`, `openOODA/lsp/`, `openOODA/mcp/`, `openOODA/opm/`): drivers, servers, and the workflow surface. No stdlib or compiler internals.
- **Tactical Code Physics** (`openOODA/std/`, `openOODA/ooda/`): the actual code. `std/` holds the 8-domain standard library; `ooda/` is the compiler + runtime monorepo (oodac, runtime C shims, smoke tests, fixtures).
- **Meta** (`openOODA/.github/`, `openOODA/website/`): org profile and website source.

Governance modifications must not trigger compiler rebuild cascades.
Compiler modifications must not alter governance process boards.
The directory topology remains stationary: `Delta_path = 0`.
This is the 3-Layer Law. See openOODA/rfcs/0004-engineering-model.oot for the full formulation.

### 1.24 Versioning Discipline — Tag, VERSION, and Release are One Number
The version number is one number, in one place: the `VERSION` file at the root of each repo.
The git tag is the *name* of that number (prefixed with `v`).
The GitHub release is the *publish* of that number.

The three artifacts — the `<repo>=X.Y.Z` line, the `release_tag=X.Y.Z` assertion, and the
`vX.Y.Z` git tag — must agree. Drift between any two is a versioning violation, not a
soft warning. The next agent or human who picks up the repo will assume the drift is
intentional and ship on top of it.

Operational rule: never tag without bumping `VERSION`; never bump `VERSION` without tagging;
never ship a release whose title does not match the tag. The order is fixed.
See `openOODA/rfcs/0006-versioning-and-release-protocol.oot` for the spec, and
`openOODA/practices/release.oot` for the workflow. The runtime check that enforces
this is `openOODA/scripts/release_invariant.oo`.

## 2. Planning Rules

  Focus: Select at most 5 tasks during a single execution turn.
  File size: Keep all files strictly <= 256 lines.
  Sprint alignment: Select active tasks from the open RFC backlog and accepted RFCs in
  `openOODA/rfcs/`. The polyrepo has no central ROADMAP.oot; each repo owns its sprint.
  Execution rhythm: Follow the Observe-Orient-Decide-Act cycle in `practices/ooda-workflow.oot`.
  Task prioritization:
    1. Fix broken tests and compilation errors first.
    2. Fix security vulnerabilities second (see openOODA/rfcs/0001-capability-security.oot).
    3. Implement scheduled features from accepted RFCs third.
    4. Refactor and clean up code last.
